data.content:
Here are the three EF Core 10 breaking changes affecting your code:

---

## 1. `ExecuteUpdateAsync` — no longer accepts expression trees

The setters parameter changed from `Expression<Func<...>>` to `Func<...>`. Your manual expression tree code won't compile.

**Fix** — replace with a simple lambda containing imperative logic:

```csharp
await context.Blogs.ExecuteUpdateAsync(s =>
{
    s.SetProperty(b => b.Views, 8);
    if (nameChanged)
    {
        s.SetProperty(b => b.Name, "foo");
    }
});
```

---

## 2. Complex type column names are now uniquified + nested paths use full prefix

Two changes hit you:
- **Duplicate names** (e.g. `ShippingAddress.Street` & `BillingAddress.Street`) now get a numeric suffix instead of silently sharing a column.
- **Nested complex types** now include the full navigation path (`Payment_BillingAddress_City` instead of `BillingAddress_City`).

**Fix** — pin explicit column names to preserve your existing schema:

```csharp
modelBuilder.Entity<Customer>(b =>
{
    b.ComplexProperty(c => c.ShippingAddress, p =>
        p.Property(a => a.Street).HasColumnName("ShippingAddress_Street"));
    b.ComplexProperty(c => c.BillingAddress, p =>
        p.Property(a => a.Street).HasColumnName("BillingAddress_Street"));
});

// For nested complex types, pin the old (short) name:
modelBuilder.Entity<Order>()
    .ComplexProperty(o => o.Payment)
    .ComplexProperty(p => p.BillingAddress)
    .Property(a => a.City)
    .HasColumnName("BillingAddress_City");
```

---

## 3. `IRelationalCommandDiagnosticsLogger` — new `logCommandText` parameter

All methods like `CommandReaderExecuting` now require an additional `string logCommandText` parameter (contains redacted SQL for logging).

**Fix:**

```csharp
public class MyLogger : IRelationalCommandDiagnosticsLogger
{
    public InterceptionResult<DbDataReader> CommandReaderExecuting(
        IRelationalConnection connection,
        DbCommand command,
        DbContext context,
        Guid commandId,
        Guid connectionId,
        DateTimeOffset startTime,
        string logCommandText)   // ← new parameter
    {
        // Use logCommandText for logging (sensitive data redacted)
        // Use command.CommandText for execution logic
    }
}
```

---

**Summary:** The expression-tree removal (#1) is the biggest simplification — you can delete all your `Expression.Lambda`/`Expression.Call` plumbing. The other two are straightforward signature/schema fixes.
